  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Save the Children Zanzibar, 2011 - 2013</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="SV">Save the Children Zanzibar, 2011 - 2013</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Save the Children Programme on Strengthening the Multi-sectoral   national child protection system in Zanzibar aims to ensure that all  children especially 10% of the most vulnerable children in Zanzibar   are protected from all forms of abuse and exploitation by 2014        through implementation of a state wide multi-sectoral collaboration   with civil society.</narrative>
   <narrative xml:lang="SV">The Save the Children programme on &quot;Establishing a Multi-stakeholder Child Protection System in Zanzibar has been funded by the Embassy of Sweden since 2011. Annual reports and various field visits done by the Programme Officers, Controllers and Political Officer have shown that there is a clear direction towards achieveing the objectives set at both outcome and impact level. There are significant and very relevant qualitative results at both the national, district and Shehia level. It is clear that the Government and other key stakeholders are recognising the benefits of the multisectoral approach in addressing child protection matters.

Furthermore, the project has demonstrated that the Government is consistently and gradually
taking over coordination and ownership of the child protection mechanisms. The programme is
suggested to be extended for the period January - June 2015  in order to provide an opportunity for a development of a comprehensive child rights governanace and child protection programme and systematic phaseout of the current support. During this period the key focus will be on strengthening the enabling environment for sustainability of the programme interventions through more focused advocacy activities, strengthening accountability and child safeguarding and children&apos;s councils, documentation of results and more indepth work with media partners to promote increased knowledge about child rights and legal provisions for general public, victims and potential victims.</narrative>
  </description>
